***********
Main Files:
***********

mm.{c,h}	
	Your solution malloc package. mm.c is the file that you
	will be handing in, and is the only file you should modify.

mdriver.c	
	The malloc driver that tests your mm.c file

short{1,2}-bal.rep
	Two tiny tracefiles to help you get started. 

Makefile	
	Builds the driver

**********************************
Other support files for the driver
**********************************

config.h	Configures the malloc lab driver
fsecs.{c,h}	Wrapper function for the different timer packages
clock.{c,h}	Routines for accessing the Pentium and Alpha cycle counters
fcyc.{c,h}	Timer functions based on cycle counters
ftimer.{c,h}	Timer functions based on interval timers and gettimeofday()
memlib.{c,h}	Models the heap and sbrk function

*******************************
Building and running the driver
*******************************
To build the driver, type "make" to the shell.

To run the driver on a tiny test trace:

	unix> mdriver -V -f short1-bal.rep

The -V option prints out helpful tracing and summary information.

To get a list of the driver flags:

	unix> mdriver -h

*******************************
Native macOS / ARM64 setup
*******************************
Run these commands from this directory:

    make
    make test

The Makefile builds for the host architecture and stores new object files
in build/, separately from the original 32-bit ELF objects. Timing uses
the existing gettimeofday backend; no x86 emulation is needed.

The default test reads all 11 traces in ./traces/ and checks alignment,
heap bounds, overlapping payloads, and realloc data preservation. It also
reports space utilization and throughput. A correctness failure produces
a nonzero exit status. The supplied mm.c is unchanged: its naive allocator
does not reuse freed blocks and runs out of heap on several full traces.

For a small debugging run:

    ./mdriver -V -f short1-bal.rep
    ./mdriver -V -f short2-bal.rep

Use the full suite when evaluating your allocator. Scores on tiny traces
are not representative. The original 60/40 utilization/throughput formula
and 600 Kops/sec throughput cap are retained, so speed points are easy to
max out on modern hardware. Compare utilization and raw Kops/sec locally;
the score is not calibrated to the original grading machine. Native pointers
and size_t are 8 bytes on ARM64; allocator metadata must account for that.

Trace provenance is documented in traces/README.md.

